<EnrolledLayout title="Family Handbook | Wild Pear Preschool & Summer Camp">
	<LegalPage2
		title="School Age Summer Camp Handbook"
		description="Everything you need to know to help your camper's time with us run smoothly."
		pubDate="May 1, 2026"
		pubDateLabel="Last Updated"
		bodyHeadings
	>
		<p>
			We are so excited for your child to be joining us for camp this summer. Whether your
			child will be with us for one week or all summer, please read the critical information below
			to help their time with us run smoothly!
		</p>

		<h2>Things to Bring</h2>
		<p>Each day your child needs to bring a bag/backpack with the following items:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>A full water bottle</li>
			<li>A healthy lunch</li>
			<li>Sunscreen they can self-apply</li>
			<li>A full change of clothes</li>
			<li>Any weather gear they may need — hoodie for chilly mornings, sun hat, etc.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Please leave toys, jewelry, art materials, and other personal items at home — they're easily
			lost or broken, and can be distracting during camp.
		</p>

		<h2>Daily Schedule</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>7:30-8:30</strong><br />
			<strong>Drop-off.</strong> Sign your child in at the binder in the covered area (where kids
			hang their coats) and say goodbye.<br />
			See the <a href="#drop-off">Drop-off</a> section for additional details.
		</p>
		<AlertWarning title="Need extra time?">
			If you need to turn in paperwork, check in with a teacher, or have extra support for your
			child during separation, please plan to arrive no later than 8:00 so we can support you.
		</AlertWarning>
		<p>
			<strong>8:30</strong><br />
			<strong>Morning circle.</strong> Campers come together to sing our way into the morning and do
			a bit of planning for the day, which will be a mix of engaging theme work and exciting free
			play activities that allow children the freedom to "pick their own adventure" of summer fun.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>9:00</strong><br />
			<strong>Morning snack.</strong> We provide a snack for all children.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>12:00</strong><br />
			<strong>Lunch.</strong> We break for lunch around noon.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>After lunch</strong><br />
			<strong>Sunscreen and water.</strong> Children are reminded to reapply sunscreen and refill
			water bottles before we get back to our work.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>2:00</strong><br />
			<strong>Closing circle.</strong> We share and reflect, and each child gets to vote for which
			child they noticed being especially kind that day.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>2:30-3:00</strong><br />
			<strong>Pick-up.</strong> Campers not attending aftercare are picked up during this window.<br />
			See the <a href="#pick-up">Pick-up</a> section for additional details.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>3:00-5:00</strong><br />
			<strong>Aftercare.</strong> Aftercare begins at 3pm and runs until 5pm. Children may be picked
			up anytime within that window. Students staying for aftercare receive a healthy snack at
			3pm.
		</p>

		<h2>Communication During the Day</h2>
		<p>
			Texting is the most convenient way for us to touch base with you during camp. Please text the
			directors on our shared family text line — that number is provided directly to enrolled
			families. For longer messages, email is a great choice. We regularly send out group emails
			that will have information that is critical for all families to know, so please make sure
			emails from us aren't headed to your spam folder.
		</p>

		<h2>Summer Weather</h2>
		<h3>Heat</h3>
		<p>
			Summer days are glorious here, and the combination of lush shade and a breeze off the creek
			creates a play space cool enough even on the hottest of days. As temperatures rise, we
			encourage children to play in the shade, drink lots of water, and keep cool by engaging in
			water play or using a cooling towel.
		</p>
		<AlertWarning title="No Air Conditioning">
			Our buildings do not have AC, and going inside to "cool off" isn't an option we have. If you
			feel the temperature is too extreme for your child, you are always welcome to pick them up
			early.
		</AlertWarning>
		<h3>Smoke</h3>
		<p>
			During wildfire season, our <dfn>air quality index (AQI)</dfn> can be dramatically impacted. If
			the AQI
			is 150-200 (red), we will be open for a half day only (7:30-12:30), with pick-up directly
			after lunch. If the AQI is above 200 (purple or maroon), we will be closed. If the school is
			in a Level 1 evacuation status due to wildfires, we will remain open but alert, and will close
			immediately if we move to a Level 2 evacuation status. If the evacuation status jumps suddenly
			to a Level 3 while children are in care, we will evacuate with them following our emergency
			plan detailed below.
		</p>
		<p>We will send an email with any closure information as needed.</p>

		<h2>Wild Pear Campus</h2>
		<p>
			Please help us protect the plants and wildlife on the property by staying on the paths
			(graveled/bark-chipped/paved spaces) while outside the play yards. Because areas outside the
			play yards are less maintained, we often have insects and plants living there which may not be
			ideal for children to wander into. There are also 2 sets of outside stairs between the upper
			and lower yard of the preschool that are not maintained, not licensed, and thus not available
			for families to use — please walk on the gravel path or the right of way in front of the house
			near the street instead.
		</p>

		<AlertWarning title="Adult Supervision Required">
			No children under age 5 are allowed to move around the campus without being directly
			accompanied by an adult. This includes going between sites, going inside to use the bathroom,
			or walking to their car. Also, please never leave a child under age 5 in your car, even if
			you're nearby and just need to snag a sibling real quick. You are welcome to let yourself in
			the gates and doors as needed any time your child is scheduled to be there.
		</AlertWarning>

		<h3>Parking</h3>
		<p>
			Any time you are here, you are welcome to park in our small lot, in two side-by-side
			driveways, or on our side of the street. (The neighbor across the street can get a little
			fussy about people parking in front of his house, and it's important to us to keep a good
			relationship with him.) You can also park in the church parking lot.
		</p>

		<h3>Play Yards</h3>
		<p>We have 3 large fenced play yards on campus:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				The "Lower Yard," which is to the right of the blue house (9411), and can be accessed from
				the gate at the small parking lot, or by walking down and around behind the blue house
				after entering from the gate on the right at the end of the driveways.
			</li>
			<li>
				The "Sprouts Yard," which is directly behind the white house (9433) and can be accessed
				through the left gate at the end of the driveways.
			</li>
			<li>
				The "School Age Yard," which is behind the Sprouts Yard, has an entrance near the teal
				garage that can be accessed by walking straight back after entering from the gate on the
				right at the end of the driveways, or by walking up and around after entering from the gate
				at the parking lot.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h2 id="drop-off">Drop-off</h2>
		<p>
			Each morning between 7:30 and 8:30 a teacher will be waiting for you inside one of our play
			yards. Please make sure you both sign your child in on the attendance sheet and make sure a
			teacher knows you are passing off your child (this can be done simply with eye contact and a
			wave, or by conversation, greeting, fancy handshake, etc). Morning drop-offs will almost always
			be in the Lower Yard.
		</p>
		<AlertWarning title="Walking Field Trips">
			Throughout the week there will be many opportunities for walking field trips in the
			neighborhood. If there is a plan to have a walking field trip that day, there will be a note
			in the sign-in area notifying parents.
		</AlertWarning>

		<h2 id="pick-up">Pick-up</h2>
		<p>
			Please plan to pick up your child between 2:30 and 3:00. Most likely younger children (K-1st)
			will be in the Lower Yard and older children (2nd-5th) will be in the School Age Yard. It's so
			important for us to make sure children are safe and connected to their grown-ups at the end of
			the day, so similar to drop-off, you will need to both sign your child out on the attendance
			sheet and make sure a teacher knows you are leaving with your child. Children will only be released to custodial
			parents and/or legal guardians unless you specify otherwise on the sign-in sheet for the day.
			In order to give families more flexibility during emergencies or unexpected circumstances, we
			also allow parents to call or text us if an emergency last-minute authorization is needed.
		</p>
		<AlertWarning title="Late?">
			If your child is not picked up on time, you will be charged $1.00 for every minute past
			3:00/5:00 that your child remains in care. That time will be based on the teacher's clock. You
			will need to pay the fee before your child can attend camp the following day.
		</AlertWarning>
		<p>
			We know it's tempting to stick around and play or chat with friends after pick-up, however, to
			remain in accordance with our liability insurance, families need to leave campus after picking
			up their children and cannot stay and play in unoccupied play yards, in parking lots/driveways,
			or other parts of campus.
		</p>

		<h2>Behavior Expectations</h2>
		<p>
			In general, we are expecting school-age campers to be willing and able to take care of their
			own basic needs, including eating and drinking, using the toilet, changing their
			clothes/layering, applying sunscreen, and keeping track of their personal belongings. If you
			think your child may need some extra support with something, please let us know so we can make
			sure to have a plan in place to help them be successful!
		</p>
		<p>
			Campers will be well supervised while they are with us, and at the same time they will have
			lots of creative freedom and physical space. Because of this, we count on campers to
			communicate with staff about any small injury, illness, or social/emotional experiences they
			may need our support with. While children will have quite a bit of flexibility to choose the
			path their day will take, there will be times when they are required to transition with the
			group in order to remain well supervised, including being nearby during meals, group meetings,
			or participating in walking field trips.
		</p>
		<p>
			At Wild Pear we work hard to create safe and inclusive environments where children, families,
			and staff are treated with dignity and respect. We recognize that being a human, especially a
			young one, comes with all kinds of feelings and needs, and our goal is to meet folks where they
			are and support them gently through the ups and downs of experiencing life together.
			Unfortunately, due to programmatic and monetary restraints, we can't always meet the needs of
			every child, and we don't have the resources to provide one-on-one care, or contained safe
			spaces for children to process and move through overwhelming feelings and severe
			dysregulation. If you are concerned about your child's specific mental, social, or emotional
			needs being met, please let us know right away, as we will need to have a plan in place before
			your child can attend camp.
		</p>
		<p>
			We expect all children and adults to treat each other in a safe and respectful manner at all
			times while at camp. This means no violence or threats of violence, no hate speech or name
			calling, and a demonstrated willingness to follow teacher directions and resolve conflict
			through respectful communication. Children who are struggling with this will be asked to take
			some developmentally appropriate space to re-regulate. Depending on the severity of the
			situation and our capacity to support your child's needs at the time, we may contact you and/or
			ask you to come pick up your child. If your child demonstrates a pattern of concerning
			behavior, we may schedule a collaboration meeting with you to try and find workable solutions
			to keep your child enrolled in camp. If no successful solution can be found, we may need to
			unenroll your child for the remainder of camp.
		</p>

		<h2>Health Policy</h2>
		<p>
			If your child has any allergies, health concerns, special needs, or other conditions we need
			to be aware of, we need you to email us right away so we can determine if we can have a plan
			and required staffing in place to support your child while they are at camp. Children with
			allergies, health concerns, special needs, etc. cannot be at camp until they have an approved
			plan of care/accommodation.
		</p>
		<p>
			Health and safety is a priority at Wild Pear Preschool, and all of our staff are trained in
			pediatric first aid and CPR. We will be playing hard outside all summer and will encourage
			children to run, jump, climb, build, and use a variety of child-appropriate tools, which may
			mean that your child will obtain small cuts and scrapes, bumps or bruises while in our care. In
			this case, we will encourage your child to wash the area with soap and water and will offer a
			Band-Aid with or without Bactine. We can also offer ice packs and/or cold towels. In the case of
			a more serious injury, the child complaining of increasing discomfort after injury, or if they
			are unable to participate in our regular activities, we will contact you right away.
		</p>
		<p>
			We do require all children to be up to date on their required immunizations and have their
			COVID immunization. If your child is sick they should stay home. Children who develop a cough,
			runny nose, sneezing, headache, or sore throat must have a negative COVID test before returning
			to camp. Masking to avoid spreading the illness to others is encouraged but not required. If
			your child tests positive for COVID, they must remain out of camp for days 1-5 and can be at
			camp wearing a mask for days 6-10. Children who are exposed to COVID (including positive
			household members) can continue coming to camp as long as they are testing negative, do not
			develop symptoms, and are properly wearing a mask.
		</p>
		<AlertWarning title="When to Stay Home">
			Children with a fever, vomiting, diarrhea, or an unexplained rash must stay home until they
			have been symptom-free for a full 24 hours without the use of medications.
		</AlertWarning>

		<h2>Medication Policy</h2>
		<p>
			Wild Pear does not administer prescribed or over-the-counter medication, with the exception of
			rescue medication (inhalers, EpiPens, Benadryl, etc). We also do not allow children to
			self-administer medications. Parents are more than welcome to come by and administer any
			medication needed throughout the day.
		</p>
		<AlertWarning title="No Medications at Camp">
			It is important that no medications, other than rescue medications, come to camp with your
			child.
		</AlertWarning>
		<p>
			If your child has a prescribed rescue medication, please contact us right away. We need to
			have a plan in place, which includes a signed form from your child's doctor, before your child
			is able to attend camp. Any rescue medication that comes to camp needs to be given to a teacher
			and kept in the first aid backpack. Let us know if you'd like us to consider an exception for
			an older child whose doctors have allowed them to self-carry and self-administer rescue meds.
		</p>

		<h2>Plan for Meeting Potential Emergencies</h2>
		<p>
			We will be alert to emergency warnings, and will follow the instructions of law enforcement and
			other designated authorities. We will use our training along with our best judgment to assess
			the situation and make the choices needed to keep ourselves and the children in our care safe
			and healthy until they can be reunited with their parents. In the event of any
			disaster/emergency, we will first contact emergency services and second we will call and text
			all custodial parents. If phones are not working we will send emails and post updates on
			Facebook.
		</p>
		<h3>Medical Emergency</h3>
		<p>
			A medical emergency is an event where one or more children need urgent medical care from a
			hospital. We will first contact emergency services, perform first aid/CPR on the child having
			the emergency, and ensure the safety of the other children in care. As soon as we are able we
			will call the child's parents and notify them of the emergency. We will ensure that either a
			teacher or a parent remains with the child at all times, including if the child needs to be
			transported to the hospital. The other teachers will remain with the group of children, keeping
			them safe and calm.
		</p>
		<h3>Shelter in Place</h3>
		<p>
			These are events that make it safer to remain sheltered inside than to be outside or evacuate,
			and may include severe storms, dangerous person threat, chemical/radiation exposure, power
			outage, and earthquakes. All parents will be notified. Teachers will remain with students on
			site until they are picked up by a parent or authorized adult. Teachers will have access to
			attendance records, emergency paperwork, rescue medication, a first aid kit, and an emergency
			kit that includes food and water, temporary toilet, child activities, and other emergency
			supplies. We will shelter in place upstairs in the main classroom, unless it is safer to be in
			the basement.
		</p>
		<h3>Evacuation</h3>
		<p>
			These are events that make it safer to leave the main school building, and may include flood,
			fire, gas leak, dangerous person threat, chemical/radiation exposure, and earthquakes. All
			parents will be notified. We will exit the school and bring attendance records, a first aid
			kit, and our emergency kit with us.
		</p>
		<h4 id="short-term-evacuation" class="pl-4">Short Term Evacuation</h4>
		<p class="pl-4">
			The entire group will move out to the studio and/or the greenhouse. We will remain there until
			it is safe to re-enter the building and/or the children are picked up by their parents.
		</p>
		<h4 class="pl-4">Long Term Evacuation</h4>
		<p class="pl-4">
			We will post a note on the door explaining where we have evacuated to. We will walk to the <a
				href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Wichita+Center+6031+SE+King+Rd+Milwaukie+OR+97222"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noopener noreferrer">Wichita Center</a
			> (6031 SE King Rd, Milwaukie, OR 97222). If we need to urgently evacuate from the wider area,
			we will load our emergency supplies and all children into our personal vehicles and drive to a
			shelter designated for the emergency, where we will contact parents and remain with the
			children until they are all reunited with their parents or authorized pick-up people.
		</p>
		<h3>Earthquake</h3>
		<p>
			We will drop and take cover until the shaking stops and it is safe to move, then follow the <a
				href="#short-term-evacuation">Short Term Evacuation</a
			> policy.
		</p>
		<h3>Lock Down</h3>
		<p>
			This is an escalated dangerous person threat whereby we believe the children are in direct
			danger. All parents will be notified. One teacher will move all children inside the school,
			turn off the lights, and close the windows. The other teacher will lock all the doors and
			windows and get the first aid kit, attendance book, and emergency kit before joining the group.
		</p>

		<h2>Mandated Reporting</h2>
		<p>
			All teachers and directors are legally required to report concerns of child abuse and neglect.
			This includes children under age 5 being left alone in a car for any reason.
		</p>

		<h2>File a Complaint</h2>
		<p>
			We are licensed as a Certified Family Child Care and we abide by the applicable Oregon State
			Rules and Regulations. We have a copy of the rules on hand if you'd like to review it. You can
			also find them online via a link to the Oregon Early Learning Website on the Resources page of
			our website. If you have a question, complaint, or a problem, we would love to have a
			discussion with you. If you do not feel comfortable with that and feel that it is serious, you
			may make an official complaint to the Office of Child Care's Central Office at 1-800-556-6616.
			You will speak to a compliance specialist who will take your information and refer it to a
			local child care licensing specialist to follow up on. You may also check our program's
			complaint and compliance history by following a link listed on the Resources page of our
			website.
		</p>
	</LegalPage2>
</EnrolledLayout>
